  "articleBody": "Ever look at newly installed window casing and feel like something’s just... not right? You’re not alone! A common culprit is a rushed measurement or skipping that crucial 'reveal' step. The reveal is that small, consistent offset from the window jamb that frames the window just so. Without it, your casing can look clunky or even cover too much of the window. Another big mistake is not gluing miter joints; nails alone just don’t cut it for a lasting, tight seam. And if your window itself wasn’t plumb, level, and square to begin with, no amount of perfect trim work will truly fix it. You’ll just be trimming over a problem! Sometimes the wall itself is uneven, making consistent casing a real challenge. You really need a stable, true surface for trim to look its best. If you’re seeing these issues, it might be time to bring in someone who can properly assess the underlying problem, whether it’s the window installation itself or a trickier wall situation. Don't just trim over a problem, fix it first!",
